+Throughout, ``leftmost'' refers to the order of the redexes by the position
+of their first symbol, \ie the pre-order on positions $p \prec q$
+($p$ is a proper prefix of $q$, or $p$ is to the left of $q$ at the first point where they differ).
+Thus $\redex_0$ is the leftmost coarse step for $(\redseq,\tm')$ when $\redex_0 \prec \redextwo$
+for every other coarse step $\redextwo$ for $(\redseq,\tm')$.
+We will use two facts about contracting a step $\redextwo$ at position $r$:
+\begin{itemize}
+\item A step at position $p$ outside the argument of $\redextwo$
+  has exactly one residual: at $p$ itself if $r$ is not a prefix of $p$,
+  and at $r\,x$ if $p = r\,0\,0\,x$ lies inside the body of the abstraction.
+  So if $p \prec q$ and neither lies in the argument of $\redextwo$,
+  then their residuals are still ordered by $\prec$, and the same holds whenever $p \prec r$.
+\item A step created by $\redextwo$ lies at a position having $r$ as a prefix,
+  or at the parent of $r$ (when $\redextwo$ is in function position).
+\end{itemize}
+
